<%# The numbers a chart draws, as a table. #337.

    Five charts rendered as a bare <canvas> with no accessible name, no description and no
    text equivalent. WCAG 1.1.1 asks for one, and a screen reader announced nothing at all --
    not even that a chart was there. /volume is a page whose whole purpose is four charts, so
    to a reader it was four summary numbers followed by silence, with no indication that
    anything was missing.

    **A table rather than an aria-label summarising the trend**, which is what #337 asks for
    and is the same split this project draws everywhere else: a summary is the app deciding
    what the chart means, and a table is the reader deciding. "Tonnage is trending up" is a
    claim; twelve numbers are what the claim was made from.

    In a <details>, closed, so it is one line for everybody and the whole table for anybody
    who wants it. Not visually hidden: a table of numbers is useful to people who can see the
    chart too -- reading an exact value off a bar is guesswork -- and something on screen is
    something that gets noticed when it goes wrong, which a hidden one never is.

    The canvas beside it is aria-hidden. It carries nothing this does not, and a screen
    reader announcing an unlabelled canvas before reading the table would be announcing
    furniture. Chartkick renders the canvas itself, so the attribute goes on a wrapper.

    `rows` is [label, value] pairs, which is the shape Volume.chart and Volume.by_exercise
    already return -- so nothing had to be reshaped to make this possible, and there is no
    second source of truth to drift. %>
<details class="mt-2">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer text-xs text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-700">
    Show as a table
  </summary>
  <table class="mt-2 w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
    <caption class="sr-only"><%= caption %></caption>
    <thead>
      <tr class="border-b border-gray-300 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">
        <th scope="col" class="py-1 pr-3 font-medium"><%= label_heading %></th>
        <th scope="col" class="py-1 font-medium"><%= value_heading %></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
      <%- rows.each do |label, value| %>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="py-1 pr-3 font-normal text-gray-700"><%= label %></th>
          <td class="py-1 tabular-nums text-gray-900"><%= chart_number(value) %></td>
        </tr>
      <%- end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</details>
